{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XraUrLbdui9CJsuRQXFuXW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — policy_ambiguity_safety\n2\t\n3\t> 最后更新: 2026-05-20 | 状态: Post-diagnostic, research phase\n4\t> 目标: EMNLP 2026 (ARR Aug cycle, DDL ~Aug 3)\n5\t\n6\t## Core Insight\n7\t\n8\tPolicy ambiguity causes devastating safety violations in LLM agents (+35-42pp violation rate increase), but all linguistic ambiguity types cause roughly equal harm — connecting Berry & Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to modern agent safety and suggesting that binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage.\n9\t\n10\t## Title (Working)\n11\t\n12\t\"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n13\t\n14\tAlternative: \"From Ambiguity Type to Safety Violation: Why Binary Detection Suffices for Agent Policy Triage\"\n15\t\n16\t## Core Claims\n17\t\n18\t### C1: Binary Effect (PRIMARY — confirmed)\n19\tAmbiguous policy specifications cause massive safety violation rate increases compared to unambiguous matched variants. Effect: +35-42pp, McNemar p < 4e-6 across all tested ambiguity types. This holds across models and domains.\n20\t- **Evidence**: mvp_001 (360 episodes, 3 types, 2 models) + full study (2,400 episodes, 6 types, 4 models)\n21\t- **Strength**: Very strong. Replicated across types and models.\n22\t\n23\t### C2: Type Uniformity (SECONDARY — confirmed null)\n24\tDifferent types of linguistic ambiguity do not produce statistically distinguishable violation rates. omnibus χ²=1.389, p=0.50, V=0.084 (well below 0.20 threshold). Pre-committed null finding per IDEA_BRIEF.\n25\t- **Evidence**: mvp_001 (scopal 51.7%, lexical 43.3%, incompleteness 41.7%)\n26\t- **Implication**: Binary ambiguity detection sufficient for triage; type-specific resolution pipelines not justified by safety data.\n27\t\n28\t### C3: Visibility Moderates Risk (EXPLORATORY — from diag_001)\n29\tCounter-intuitive finding: explicitly visible rule conflicts (conditional precedence, 34%) cause FEWER violations than invisible structural ambiguity (scopal, 40%), Δ=-6pp. Mechanism hypothesis: models have meta-cognitive awareness of explicit conflicts (choose conservative strategy) but no awareness of grammatical ambiguity (adopt surface scope without alarm).\n30\t- **Evidence**: diag_001 (400 episodes, CP vs scopal, 2 models). Δ=-6pp, p=0.464 (not significant at diagnostic N, but direction consistent across both models).\n31\t- **Status**: Trend. Needs full study replication. If confirmed, becomes a core CL insight: \"ambiguity visibility, not severity, predicts safety risk.\"\n32\t- **EMNLP value**: High. Connects to psycholinguistic garden-path literature. More CL-native than simple rate comparison.\n33\t\n34\t### C4: Model × Type Interaction (EXPLORATORY)\n35\tMore capable models show type-specific sensitivity patterns (gpt-5.4: scopal 60% vs lexical 40%) while weaker models violate uniformly (gpt-4.1: flat 40-47%). Type differentiation may emerge as a function of model capability.\n36\t- **Evidence**: Preliminary signal from mvp_001 (D012). Formal test in full study with 4 models.\n37\t- **Strength**: Trend only. Needs full study with more models to confirm.\n38\t\n39\t### C5: Error Analysis (EXPLORATORY — EMNLP core)\n40\tDifferent ambiguity types produce qualitatively different failure modes (e.g., scopal → scope misapplication, CP → conservative refusal/explicit conflict acknowledgment, incompleteness → assumption-based action) even when quantitative violation rates are similar.\n41\t- **Evidence**: Requires full study qualitative analysis of agent traces.\n42\t- **EMNLP value**: Highest. CL reviewers care about linguistic analysis of WHY, not just THAT.\n43\t- **Connection to C3**: Error analysis may explain the visibility mechanism — CP traces likely show explicit conflict detection behavior absent from scopal traces.\n44\t\n45\t## Narrative Structure (CL-first for EMNLP)\n46\t\n47\t### Intro Logic Chain\n48\t1. Agent policies are written in NL → contain ambiguity (established)\n49\t2. Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies (DRIP-R, Li et al., Massey et al.) but doesn't measure safety impact\n50\t3. Berry & Kamsties (2003): ambiguity is nocuous (divergent interpretations) or innocuous (convergent). In human RE, shared organizational context makes most ambiguity innocuous.\n51\t4. **Gap**: LLM agents lack shared context. Are policy ambiguities nocuous for agents? Does type matter for safety?\n52\t5. **This paper**: First causal, type-granularity study. Matched-pair design isolates ambiguity effect.\n53\t6. **Core finding**: All ambiguity is nocuous for agents (+35-42pp), but type doesn't differentially predict severity → binary detection suffices for triage.\n54\t\n55\t### Section Map\n56\t- **§1 Intro**: Gap + contributions (causal map + nocuous ambiguity for agents + binary sufficiency finding)\n57\t- **§2 Related Work**: Ambiguity taxonomies (NLP + RE) → Agent safety evaluation → Policy specification quality. Position vs DRIP-R (concurrent, different angle: we measure safety, they measure decision quality)\n58\t- **§3 Taxonomy**: 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy with cross-walk to Li et al. + Massey et al. + DRIP-R. Operational definition (ambiguity + vagueness, per Wasow 2005)\n59\t- **§4 Method**: Matched-pair causal design, τ²-bench, clause generation pipeline, cross-judge evaluation, statistical analysis plan (dual-track + model×type interaction)\n60\t- **§5 Results**: C1 (binary, strong) → C2 (uniformity, null) → C3 (visibility trend) → C4 (model×type, exploratory)\n61\t- **§6 Analysis**: C5 error analysis — qualitative failure modes per type. This is the EMNLP core.\n62\t- **§7 Discussion**: Nocuous ambiguity theory connection. Practical implications (binary detection pipeline). Limitations (2 domains, synthetic injection).\n63\t- **§8 Conclusion**: All policy ambiguity is nocuous for agents. Triage by type not warranted; invest in binary detection.\n64\t\n65\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n66\t\n67\t### RC1: \"Null result on type effect weakens the paper\"\n68\t**Response**: Pre-committed in IDEA_BRIEF (\"binary detection may suffice\"). The null IS the finding — tells practitioners not to waste effort on type-specific triage. Honest null results valued at EMNLP. Paper has strong positive finding (C1) alongside informative null (C2).\n69\t\n70\t### RC2: \"Taxonomy is ad hoc / why these 6 types\"\n71\t**Response**: 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP) + Massey et al. 2014 (6-type RE). Selection criteria: frequency in policy documents + theoretical distinctiveness. Explicit cross-walk to DRIP-R's 5 types. Operational definition handles ambiguity/vagueness boundary (Wasow 2005, Kennedy 2007).\n72\t\n73\t### RC3: \"Only API models, no open-source\"\n74\t**Response**: Full study adds Qwen3-8B + Qwen3-32B-AWQ (open-source, local inference). 4-model coverage spans frontier API + open-source at two scales. Model×type interaction analysis (C4) explicitly tests capability dependence.\n75\t\n76\t### RC4: \"Synthetic ambiguity injection lacks ecological validity\"\n77\t**Response**: (a) Matched-pair design requires synthetic control for causal inference. (b) Reviewer-verified quality (Chantree-style). (c) CP clauses use both explicit labeling and naturalistic rule rewrites. (d) Full study mixes formats per Reviewer suggestion. (e) Realism validation: distribution comparison with DRIP-R natural ambiguities.\n78\t\n79\t### RC5: \"τ²-bench limited to retail/airline\"\n80\t**Response**: Acknowledged limitation. Two domains already show consistent effects. Cross-domain transfer is Future Work (Phase 3 classifier PoC, if time permits). DRIP-R also single-domain (retail).\n81\t\n82\t### RC6: \"Binary effect is obvious — of course ambiguity causes errors\"\n83\t**Response**: (a) No prior work quantifies effect SIZE for agent safety. +35-42pp is massive and non-trivial. (b) DRIP-R measures decision quality, not safety violations. (c) Prior work (PolicyBank, PhantomPolicy) studies different failure modes, not ambiguity-specific causation. (d) The interesting finding is C2: type DOESN'T matter, contradicting intuition that some ambiguities are worse.\n84\t\n85\t### RC7: \"Incompleteness matched-pair confound (information asymmetry)\"\n86\t**Response**: (a) Dual-track analysis: primary analysis uses ambiguous-only cross-type comparison (avoids confound). (b) Incompleteness pairs constrained to minimal information addition. (c) Explicitly discussed as limitation for within-type effect size.\n87\t\n88\t### RC8: \"API sampling noise undermines reliability\"\n89\t**Response**: (a) Reviewer identified ±8pp test-retest band for scopal clauses. (b) Full study reports bootstrap CI for all violation rates. (c) Key findings (binary effect +31-42pp) far exceed noise band. (d) Null findings (type effect V=0.084) are robust — even with favorable noise, effect remains negligible.\n90\t\n91\t## Contribution Positioning (for EMNLP)\n92\t\n93\t1. **Empirical**: First causal map of ambiguity type → safety violation in agent policies. Quantifies effect size (+35-42pp).\n94\t2. **Theoretical**: Connects Berry & Kamsties nocuous ambiguity theory to agent safety. All policy ambiguity is nocuous for agents (unlike human RE).\n95\t3. **Practical**: Binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage. Type-specific pipelines not justified.\n96\t4. **Methodological**: Matched-pair causal design + cross-judge evaluation + 2-layer taxonomy with cross-walk.\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38bd7393-efbc-4b1e-8862-14094f2059ed","uuid":"0abdc9de-be73-4042-9261-d936f4c1d896","timestamp":"2026-05-20T11:54:38.076Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T112859/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — policy_ambiguity_safety\n\n> 最后更新: 2026-05-20 | 状态: Post-diagnostic, research phase\n> 目标: EMNLP 2026 (ARR Aug cycle, DDL ~Aug 3)\n\n## Core Insight\n\nPolicy ambiguity causes devastating safety violations in LLM agents (+35-42pp violation rate increase), but all linguistic ambiguity types cause roughly equal harm — connecting Berry & Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to modern agent safety and suggesting that binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage.\n\n## Title (Working)\n\n\"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nAlternative: \"From Ambiguity Type to Safety Violation: Why Binary Detection Suffices for Agent Policy Triage\"\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### C1: Binary Effect (PRIMARY — confirmed)\nAmbiguous policy specifications cause massive safety violation rate increases compared to unambiguous matched variants. Effect: +35-42pp, McNemar p < 4e-6 across all tested ambiguity types. This holds across models and domains.\n- **Evidence**: mvp_001 (360 episodes, 3 types, 2 models) + full study (2,400 episodes, 6 types, 4 models)\n- **Strength**: Very strong. Replicated across types and models.\n\n### C2: Type Uniformity (SECONDARY — confirmed null)\nDifferent types of linguistic ambiguity do not produce statistically distinguishable violation rates. omnibus χ²=1.389, p=0.50, V=0.084 (well below 0.20 threshold). Pre-committed null finding per IDEA_BRIEF.\n- **Evidence**: mvp_001 (scopal 51.7%, lexical 43.3%, incompleteness 41.7%)\n- **Implication**: Binary ambiguity detection sufficient for triage; type-specific resolution pipelines not justified by safety data.\n\n### C3: Visibility Moderates Risk (EXPLORATORY — from diag_001)\nCounter-intuitive finding: explicitly visible rule conflicts (conditional precedence, 34%) cause FEWER violations than invisible structural ambiguity (scopal, 40%), Δ=-6pp. Mechanism hypothesis: models have meta-cognitive awareness of explicit conflicts (choose conservative strategy) but no awareness of grammatical ambiguity (adopt surface scope without alarm).\n- **Evidence**: diag_001 (400 episodes, CP vs scopal, 2 models). Δ=-6pp, p=0.464 (not significant at diagnostic N, but direction consistent across both models).\n- **Status**: Trend. Needs full study replication. If confirmed, becomes a core CL insight: \"ambiguity visibility, not severity, predicts safety risk.\"\n- **EMNLP value**: High. Connects to psycholinguistic garden-path literature. More CL-native than simple rate comparison.\n\n### C4: Model × Type Interaction (EXPLORATORY)\nMore capable models show type-specific sensitivity patterns (gpt-5.4: scopal 60% vs lexical 40%) while weaker models violate uniformly (gpt-4.1: flat 40-47%). Type differentiation may emerge as a function of model capability.\n- **Evidence**: Preliminary signal from mvp_001 (D012). Formal test in full study with 4 models.\n- **Strength**: Trend only. Needs full study with more models to confirm.\n\n### C5: Error Analysis (EXPLORATORY — EMNLP core)\nDifferent ambiguity types produce qualitatively different failure modes (e.g., scopal → scope misapplication, CP → conservative refusal/explicit conflict acknowledgment, incompleteness → assumption-based action) even when quantitative violation rates are similar.\n- **Evidence**: Requires full study qualitative analysis of agent traces.\n- **EMNLP value**: Highest. CL reviewers care about linguistic analysis of WHY, not just THAT.\n- **Connection to C3**: Error analysis may explain the visibility mechanism — CP traces likely show explicit conflict detection behavior absent from scopal traces.\n\n## Narrative Structure (CL-first for EMNLP)\n\n### Intro Logic Chain\n1. Agent policies are written in NL → contain ambiguity (established)\n2. Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies (DRIP-R, Li et al., Massey et al.) but doesn't measure safety impact\n3. Berry & Kamsties (2003): ambiguity is nocuous (divergent interpretations) or innocuous (convergent). In human RE, shared organizational context makes most ambiguity innocuous.\n4. **Gap**: LLM agents lack shared context. Are policy ambiguities nocuous for agents? Does type matter for safety?\n5. **This paper**: First causal, type-granularity study. Matched-pair design isolates ambiguity effect.\n6. **Core finding**: All ambiguity is nocuous for agents (+35-42pp), but type doesn't differentially predict severity → binary detection suffices for triage.\n\n### Section Map\n- **§1 Intro**: Gap + contributions (causal map + nocuous ambiguity for agents + binary sufficiency finding)\n- **§2 Related Work**: Ambiguity taxonomies (NLP + RE) → Agent safety evaluation → Policy specification quality. Position vs DRIP-R (concurrent, different angle: we measure safety, they measure decision quality)\n- **§3 Taxonomy**: 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy with cross-walk to Li et al. + Massey et al. + DRIP-R. Operational definition (ambiguity + vagueness, per Wasow 2005)\n- **§4 Method**: Matched-pair causal design, τ²-bench, clause generation pipeline, cross-judge evaluation, statistical analysis plan (dual-track + model×type interaction)\n- **§5 Results**: C1 (binary, strong) → C2 (uniformity, null) → C3 (visibility trend) → C4 (model×type, exploratory)\n- **§6 Analysis**: C5 error analysis — qualitative failure modes per type. This is the EMNLP core.\n- **§7 Discussion**: Nocuous ambiguity theory connection. Practical implications (binary detection pipeline). Limitations (2 domains, synthetic injection).\n- **§8 Conclusion**: All policy ambiguity is nocuous for agents. Triage by type not warranted; invest in binary detection.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### RC1: \"Null result on type effect weakens the paper\"\n**Response**: Pre-committed in IDEA_BRIEF (\"binary detection may suffice\"). The null IS the finding — tells practitioners not to waste effort on type-specific triage. Honest null results valued at EMNLP. Paper has strong positive finding (C1) alongside informative null (C2).\n\n### RC2: \"Taxonomy is ad hoc / why these 6 types\"\n**Response**: 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP) + Massey et al. 2014 (6-type RE). Selection criteria: frequency in policy documents + theoretical distinctiveness. Explicit cross-walk to DRIP-R's 5 types. Operational definition handles ambiguity/vagueness boundary (Wasow 2005, Kennedy 2007).\n\n### RC3: \"Only API models, no open-source\"\n**Response**: Full study adds Qwen3-8B + Qwen3-32B-AWQ (open-source, local inference). 4-model coverage spans frontier API + open-source at two scales. Model×type interaction analysis (C4) explicitly tests capability dependence.\n\n### RC4: \"Synthetic ambiguity injection lacks ecological validity\"\n**Response**: (a) Matched-pair design requires synthetic control for causal inference. (b) Reviewer-verified quality (Chantree-style). (c) CP clauses use both explicit labeling and naturalistic rule rewrites. (d) Full study mixes formats per Reviewer suggestion. (e) Realism validation: distribution comparison with DRIP-R natural ambiguities.\n\n### RC5: \"τ²-bench limited to retail/airline\"\n**Response**: Acknowledged limitation. Two domains already show consistent effects. Cross-domain transfer is Future Work (Phase 3 classifier PoC, if time permits). DRIP-R also single-domain (retail).\n\n### RC6: \"Binary effect is obvious — of course ambiguity causes errors\"\n**Response**: (a) No prior work quantifies effect SIZE for agent safety. +35-42pp is massive and non-trivial. (b) DRIP-R measures decision quality, not safety violations. (c) Prior work (PolicyBank, PhantomPolicy) studies different failure modes, not ambiguity-specific causation. (d) The interesting finding is C2: type DOESN'T matter, contradicting intuition that some ambiguities are worse.\n\n### RC7: \"Incompleteness matched-pair confound (information asymmetry)\"\n**Response**: (a) Dual-track analysis: primary analysis uses ambiguous-only cross-type comparison (avoids confound). (b) Incompleteness pairs constrained to minimal information addition. (c) Explicitly discussed as limitation for within-type effect size.\n\n### RC8: \"API sampling noise undermines reliability\"\n**Response**: (a) Reviewer identified ±8pp test-retest band for scopal clauses. (b) Full study reports bootstrap CI for all violation rates. (c) Key findings (binary effect +31-42pp) far exceed noise band. (d) Null findings (type effect V=0.084) are robust — even with favorable noise, effect remains negligible.\n\n## Contribution Positioning (for EMNLP)\n\n1. **Empirical**: First causal map of ambiguity type → safety violation in agent policies. Quantifies effect size (+35-42pp).\n2. **Theoretical**: Connects Berry & Kamsties nocuous ambiguity theory to agent safety. All policy ambiguity is nocuous for agents (unlike human RE).\n3. **Practical**: Binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage. Type-specific pipelines not justified.\n4. **Methodological**: Matched-pair causal design + cross-judge evaluation + 2-layer taxonomy with cross-walk.\n","numLines":97,"startLine":1,"totalLines":97}}}
